# The text-ranges scene: HIGHLIGHT a set, SELECT one, REVEAL one, and
# the two things that make those three a contract rather than three
# calls — a user's keystroke DROPS a declared set (D2), and a
# select_range arriving mid-composition is REFUSED (D4).
#
# THE OFFSETS ARE UTF-8 BYTE OFFSETS into the document the guest opened
# (guests/rust/ranges.rs), which is kaya's unit in every binding and on
# the wire. The document's first line is CJK, so every match sits SIX
# BYTES further along than it sits in UTF-16 — the unit four of the five
# backends count. A backend that forwarded kaya's offsets as if they
# were already its own would decorate six characters early and these
# numbers would say so.
#
# EVERY RANGE ASSERTION CARRIES BOTH the offsets and the text they
# cover, `start:end=covered`. That is not belt and braces: the core
# converts bytes into the backend's unit to lower a range and the
# backend converts back to read one, so two symmetric mistakes would
# cancel and offsets alone would pass while the wrong characters were
# decorated. The covered text has no arithmetic in it — the platform
# slices its own string with the range it is holding.
#
# THIS FILE IS PURE ASCII, deliberately. The document's non-ASCII lives
# in the guest's own source, where every language's compiler guarantees
# UTF-8; a .steps file travels through three step interpreters, a shell
# and an environment variable, and none of that decoding is proven yet
# (scratchpad/ranges-units.md §8.7 asked for it). The CJK prefix buys
# the unit assertion without needing any of it.
expect label#0 "0 matches"
# NOTHING IS DECORATED YET, and asserting it is what stops the next
# assertion from being untestable: a backend that decorated everything,
# or that answered from a stale read, fails here first.
expect_highlights textarea#0 ""
click button#0
expect label#0 "3 matches"
expect_highlights textarea#0 "57:62=alpha|203:208=alpha|753:758=alpha"
# The SECOND match, so the selection is distinguishable from "the first
# thing the search found".
expect_selection textarea#0 "203:208=alpha"
# REVEAL, asserted in both directions. The `offscreen` leg is what
# makes the `visible` one mean something: on a document short enough to
# fit, containment is true before anything scrolls and the whole verb
# would pass vacuously.
expect_revealed textarea#0 753:758 offscreen
click button#1
expect_revealed textarea#0 753:758 visible
# A REVEAL IS A PURE EFFECT: it moves the viewport and nothing else, so
# the declared set is still declared and the selection has not moved.
expect_highlights textarea#0 "57:62=alpha|203:208=alpha|753:758=alpha"
expect_selection textarea#0 "203:208=alpha"
# D2 — A TEXT EDIT DROPS THE DECLARED SET. `type` goes in through the
# platform's own input path, so this is a user's keystroke and not a
# write kaya made. The set is DROPPED, not shifted: kaya tracks nothing
# across an edit, and the app re-declares from the fold text_changed
# already drives (which is what the label says).
click button#2
expect_focused textarea#0
type " z"
expect label#0 "0 matches"
expect_highlights textarea#0 ""
# D4 — A SELECTION ARRIVING MID-COMPOSITION IS REFUSED. `compose`
# leaves marked text in the control: displayed, uncommitted, and
# invisible to the app, exactly as a half-typed pinyin or kana sequence
# is. Honouring a selection now would COMMIT it into the document
# mid-word and shift every later offset — data loss shaped like a
# feature — so every backend refuses, leaves the composition alone and
# names the reason.
#
# THE OBSERVABLE IS THE CARET: a composition parks it at the end of the
# marked text, and that is where it must still be. Had the select been
# honoured, this would read `57:62=alpha`.
compose textarea#0 "nihon"
click button#3
expect_selection textarea#0 "820:820="
